  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was a grooming closure at both locations\u2014zero appointments across Port Washington and Hicksville\u2014which explains the revenue collapse (down 61% and 9% respectively) and zero grooming labor. This was a scheduled or unplanned shutdown; I need clarification on whether this was intentional, weather-related, or a staffing event. Sunday operations are inherently light, but the week-to-date picture (both locations at zero YTD appointments) signals something structural broke Monday through yesterday.\n\nThe real news is buried underneath: Port Washington is running *hot* month-to-date. At 4.3 groomer-days per day you're down 7% in groomer capacity versus July, yet you're outperforming the DOW-adjusted expectation by 6.5% ($26.4K actual vs. $24.8K expected). Revenue per groomer-day sits at $473\u2014slightly below the $500 trailing baseline\u2014but the math works because your team is booking smarter. Year-to-date you're up 45% in both revenue and appointments versus last year, which is your real scorecard. Hicksville is underperforming the prior-month baseline by 19% and 27% in appointments, and even the DOW-adjusted reality check (+3.8%) doesn't salvage the trend: you're down to 1.9 groomer-days per day from 2.6. This is a capacity problem, not an execution problem, but it's starving revenue.\n\nBoth locations face acute inventory strain\u2014treat items deeply negative, supplies at reorder threshold. Port Washington's lapse pool ($296K annual value) is material; Hicksville's is smaller but still actionable. Today you have 6 total groomer-days scheduled across both locations; tomorrow 9. Next seven days: 52 groomer-days total, but Hicksville's 18 groomer-days (versus Port Washington's 34) shows where the gap lives."
